[test-suite name=xpath-31-parity ring=1
  [doc [#
# CXPath / XPath 3.1 alignment corpus — gates 28.5a and 28.5b.
#
# Normative reference: spec/02-working/cxpath_alignment.md (authored under
# RULED: VC-7, #945; PROPOSED for graduation, NOT graduated).
#
# TWO ROWS READ THIS FILE.
#   28.5a  CX side, AUTOMATED, in TEST_TARGETS, no Docker.
#          scripts/check_xpath_parity_fixtures.cx
#          `make test-xpath-parity-cx`
#          Gate tier: conformance/gates.cxd, suite `xpath-31-parity`.
#   28.5b  Saxon-HE 12 cross-check, MANUAL by ruling.
#          scripts/test_xpath_parity.sh holds the operator recipe.
#
# Both rows read the SAME [input-xml] body, so the manual row can never
# drift onto a different document than the lane grades.
#
# SECTIONS
#   [tag]           xpath31-parity | xpath31-divergence
#   [input-xml]     the XML document. `cx select` reads a CX document, not
#                   XML, so 28.5a converts it first: `cx --from=xml --to=cx`.
#   [path]          the expression. CXPath spelling on parity cases; the
#                   XPath 3.1 spelling on the divergence cases that exist to
#                   pin a refusal.
#   [out-text]      28.5a: the exact `cx select` output in canonical CX.
#   [out-err]       28.5a: the cx-err:CXERNNNN code on a refusal. The
#                   assertion is on the CODE, never the message text —
#                   misc/cli.md makes message text explicitly unstable across
#                   patch versions while error codes carry a stability policy.
#   [expect-saxon]  28.5b: the XPath-engine result. HAND-AUTHORED AND NEVER
#                   VERIFIED against a running Saxon — verifying these is
#                   28.5b's first job, not an assumption it may rest on.
#   [expect-diff]   28.5b: prose, why the engines differ, keyed to a clause of
#                   cxpath_alignment.md.
#
# EVERY [out-text] AND [out-err] BODY IN THIS FILE WAS DERIVED BY RUNNING THE
# SHIPPED BINARY (the cx built from commit 72881ab0f, 2026-08-24). None is
# hand-authored. That is the discipline whose absence caused #945: the
# previous [out-text] bodies recorded XML (`<user id="1"/>`) while cx emits
# canonical CX (`[user id=1]`), so every case would have failed on frame
# alone, in every environment — and nobody found out, because the runner
# exited 2 before doing any work and no lane called it.
#
# TAGS AND WHAT THEY ASSERT
#   xpath31-parity      CX accepts the expression and emits exactly
#                       [out-text]; exit 0 for a non-empty expectation, 1 for
#                       an empty one (the misc/cli.md `cx select` contract).
#                       28.5b additionally asserts the same NODE SET as Saxon
#                       — never the same bytes, since the two engines
#                       serialize differently by design.
#   xpath31-divergence  CX and XPath 3.1 differ here, in the recorded way.
#                       Two shapes, both machine-checked by 28.5a:
#                         (a) with [out-err] — CX REFUSES: exit 2 and the
#                             recorded code on stderr. Saxon accepts (28.5b).
#                         (b) with [out-text] — CX ACCEPTS but produces a
#                             different result, recorded exactly. The only
#                             instance is xpath31-002 (attribute auto-typing).
#                       An accidental convergence is a regression: if Saxon
#                       ever refuses an (a)-shape case too, the divergence has
#                       evaporated and the case tests nothing.
#
# SEVEN CASES WERE RE-TAGGED parity -> divergence UNDER VC-7, because
# measurement contradicted the tag and approved normative text says the
# measurement is right, not the tag:
#   002  attribute auto-typing (shape b)
#   030  `cx select` takes a PATH expression, not an arbitrary call
#   031  032  033  the paren-call form is retired everywhere
#   041  infix pipe is retired
#   042  infix `||` is retired
# Each carries its [expect-diff] naming the clause. The corpus therefore now
# makes 12 parity claims, not 19 — a real reduction in the number of PARITY
# assertions, stated here rather than absorbed silently. The capabilities
# those seven meant to reach (aggregate and string built-ins reached through
# head dispatch inside a predicate) are NOT pinned by this corpus; that gap
# is tracked, not papered over.
#]]

 [# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    §1. Selection — basic axes (parity)
    ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #]

 [case id=xpath31-001-descendant-or-self-name
  [tag [#
xpath31-parity
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<users><user id="1"/><user id="2"/><user id="3"/></users>
#]]
  [path [#
//user
#]]
  [out-text [#
[user id=1]
[user id=2]
[user id=3]
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
<user id="1"/>
<user id="2"/>
<user id="3"/>
#]]
 ]

 [case id=xpath31-002-attribute-predicate-equality
  [tag [#
xpath31-divergence
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<users><user id="1" active="true"/><user id="2" active="false"/></users>
#]]
  [path [#
//user[= $_@active 'true']
#]]
  [out-text [#
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
<user id="1" active="true"/>
#]]
  [expect-diff [#
Saxon returns the first user: absent a schema every attribute is
xs:untypedAtomic, so @active='true' is a STRING comparison and matches.

CX accepts the expression and selects NOTHING (exit 1). xml_to_cx auto-types
attribute values, so active="true" arrives as the BOOLEAN true, and CX is
type-strict: [= true 'true'] is false. Measured end to end 2026-08-24.

This is the typed-value-layer divergence — cxpath_alignment.md, "no XSD type
layer" and "sequence flatness and the typed-value model". It is the SPECIFIED
answer on both sides, and it is the reason the alignment claim is scoped to
path SYNTAX rather than to value comparison. It is also the one divergence in
this corpus that is not a parse refusal, which is why the divergence tag
carries two shapes.
#]]
 ]

 [case id=xpath31-003-attribute-step
  [tag [#
xpath31-parity
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<users><user email="a@x.org"/><user email="b@x.org"/></users>
#]]
  [path [#
//user/@email
#]]
  [out-text [#
[email 'a@x.org']
[email 'b@x.org']
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
a@x.org
b@x.org
#]]
  [# The attribute step is spelled with its separator, `/@email` — the glued
     `//user@email` tail is binding-path surface, not a path production
     (grammar.ebnf [130a]). Attribute-axis matches materialize as [name value]
     fields per the misc/cli.md `cx select` output rule, which is why the CX
     frame is `[email 'a@x.org']` and the XPath frame is a bare string: same
     node set, different serialization. #]
 ]

 [case id=xpath31-004-positional-predicate
  [tag [#
xpath31-parity
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<doc><item n="1"/><item n="2"/><item n="3"/></doc>
#]]
  [path [#
//item[2]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[item n=2]
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
<item n="2"/>
#]]
 ]

 [case id=xpath31-005-following-sibling-axis
  [tag [#
xpath31-parity
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<doc><item n="1"/><item n="2"/><item n="3"/></doc>
#]]
  [path [#
//item[1]/following-sibling::item
#]]
  [out-text [#
[item n=2]
[item n=3]
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
<item n="2"/>
<item n="3"/>
#]]
 ]

 [case id=xpath31-006-ancestor-axis
  [tag [#
xpath31-parity
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<doc><section><para>text</para></section></doc>
#]]
  [path [#
//para/ancestor::section
#]]
  [out-text [#
[section [para 'text']]
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
<section><para>text</para></section>
#]]
 ]

 [case id=xpath31-007-self-axis-name-test
  [tag [#
xpath31-parity
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<doc><a/><b/><c/></doc>
#]]
  [path [#
//*[or [= [$name $_] "a"] [= [$name $_] "c"]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[a]
[c]
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
<a/>
<c/>
#]]
  [# The XPath spelling of this predicate would be
     `[self::a or self::c]` — infix `or` and no brackets. CX writes the same
     selection as a fused prefix form over the head-dispatch `[$name …]`
     built-in. The SELECTION is the parity claim; the SPELLING diverges by
     the homoiconic invariant (cxpath_alignment.md, the retired predicate
     sublanguage). #]
 ]

 [case id=xpath31-008-descendant-axis-explicit
  [tag [#
xpath31-parity
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<doc><x><y><z/></y></x></doc>
#]]
  [path [#
//descendant::z
#]]
  [out-text [#
[z]
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
<z/>
#]]
 ]

 [# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    §2. Predicates — comparisons
    ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #]

 [case id=xpath31-020-predicate-ge
  [tag [#
xpath31-parity
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<users><user age="20"/><user age="25"/><user age="30"/></users>
#]]
  [path [#
//user[>= $_@age 25]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[user age=25]
[user age=30]
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
<user age="25"/>
<user age="30"/>
#]]
  [# The engines agree HERE, and the agreement is partly luck: CX compares
     INTEGERS (xml_to_cx auto-typed the age="25" attribute into the integer 25) while XPath compares
     untyped atomics numerically for `>=`. They would disagree on age="025".
     Contrast xpath31-002, where the same auto-typing makes an equality
     comparison diverge. #]
 ]

 [case id=xpath31-021-predicate-not-equal
  [tag [#
xpath31-parity
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<users><user role="admin"/><user role="member"/></users>
#]]
  [path [#
//user[!= $_@role 'admin']
#]]
  [out-text [#
[user role=member]
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
<user role="member"/>
#]]
 ]

 [case id=xpath31-022-predicate-lt
  [tag [#
xpath31-parity
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<items><item price="5"/><item price="10"/><item price="15"/></items>
#]]
  [path [#
//item[< $_@price 10]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[item price=5]
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
<item price="5"/>
#]]
 ]

 [# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    §3. Built-in functions
    ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #]

 [case id=xpath31-030-count
  [tag [#
xpath31-divergence
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<users><user/><user/><user/></users>
#]]
  [path [#
[$count //user]
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0100
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
3
#]]
  [expect-diff [#
Saxon returns 3 for count(//user).

CX refuses, exit 2:
  cx select: cx-err:CXER0100: PATH must be a CXPath path expression
  ($doc/…, /… or //…), got: [$count //user]

Two separate reasons, and the distinction matters:
 (1) XPath's paren-call count(…) is retired everywhere in CX
     (cxpath_alignment.md, the retired predicate sublanguage).
 (2) The expression above is ALREADY in valid CX head-dispatch spelling, and
     `cx select` still refuses it — because misc/cli.md scopes the PATH
     argument to a single CXPath path expression ($doc-anchored, rooted, or
     descendant), and a call whose ARGUMENT is a path is not a path. So this
     case pins a CLI-SURFACE scope fact, not only an XPath divergence.
     `[$count //user]` evaluates fine as a program.
#]]
 ]

 [case id=xpath31-031-name-function
  [tag [#
xpath31-divergence
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<doc><foo/></doc>
#]]
  [path [#
name(//foo)
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0100
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
foo
#]]
  [expect-diff [#
Saxon evaluates name(//foo) to 'foo'.

CX refuses at parse, exit 2:
  paren-call `name(…)` is retired — there is no paren-call anywhere in the
  language; write the head-dispatch form `[$name …]`

There is NO paren-call at any position in CX, CXPath predicate bodies
included (cxpath_alignment.md, the retired predicate sublanguage). The
capability is reached head-dispatch; xpath31-007 exercises `[$name $_]`
inside a predicate.
#]]
 ]

 [case id=xpath31-032-string-length
  [tag [#
xpath31-divergence
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<doc><greeting>hello</greeting></doc>
#]]
  [path [#
string-length(//greeting)
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0100
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
5
#]]
  [expect-diff [#
Saxon returns 5 for string-length(//greeting).

CX refuses at parse, exit 2: the paren-call form is retired; the head-dispatch
spelling is `[$string-length …]`. Same clause as xpath31-031.
#]]
 ]

 [case id=xpath31-033-contains
  [tag [#
xpath31-divergence
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<doc><user email="alice@example.org"/></doc>
#]]
  [path [#
contains(//user/@email, 'example')
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0100
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
true
#]]
  [expect-diff [#
Saxon evaluates contains(//user/@email, 'example') to true.

CX refuses at parse, exit 2: the paren-call form is retired; the head-dispatch
spelling is `[$contains …]`. Same clause as xpath31-031.
#]]
 ]

 [case id=xpath31-034-position
  [tag [#
xpath31-parity
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<doc><item n="1"/><item n="2"/><item n="3"/></doc>
#]]
  [path [#
//item[2]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[item n=2]
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
<item n="2"/>
#]]
  [# The positional notation atom [N] is spelled identically in both
     languages and means `$_position = N` (grammar.ebnf [159a]). Same shape
     as xpath31-004 by design — 004 pins it as a predicate form, 034 pins it
     as the position() equivalent. #]
 ]

 [case id=xpath31-035-last
  [tag [#
xpath31-parity
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<doc><item n="1"/><item n="2"/><item n="3"/></doc>
#]]
  [path [#
//item[= $_position $_last]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[item n=3]
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
<item n="3"/>
#]]
  [# XPath writes this `[position() = last()]`. CX's reserved predicate
     bindings $_position / $_last are the context accessors, and the
     comparison is the prefix operator form. #]
 ]

 [# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    §4. Sequence operators
    ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #]

 [# xpath31-040-range REMOVED — the XPath 3.1 `to` range operator is retired
    from CX (the no-infix / homoiconic invariant; ranges are the prefix builtin
    [$range lo hi step?], generator_family_reshape.md). CX deliberately does not
    claim Saxon parity on infix `to`; the operator is a parse error everywhere. #]

 [case id=xpath31-041-union
  [tag [#
xpath31-divergence
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<doc><a id="1"/><b id="2"/><a id="3"/></doc>
#]]
  [path [#
//a | //b
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0100
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
<a id="1"/>
<b id="2"/>
<a id="3"/>
#]]
  [expect-diff [#
Saxon evaluates the union and returns all three elements in document order.

CX refuses at parse, exit 2:
  unexpected token '|' in expression position

grammar.ebnf is explicit: "Pipe `|` is retired (use the prefix
combinators)". The CAPABILITY exists — `[union A B]` is a reserved prefix
head valid in every expression position — but it is not reachable through
the `cx select` PATH argument, for the same surface-scope reason as
xpath31-030. So the SET-COMBINATION capability aligns and the SPELLING
diverges (cxpath_alignment.md, set combination).
#]]
 ]

 [case id=xpath31-042-string-concat
  [tag [#
xpath31-divergence
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<doc/>
#]]
  [path [#
'foo' || '-' || 'bar'
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0100
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
foo-bar
#]]
  [expect-diff [#
Saxon evaluates the concatenation to 'foo-bar'.

CX refuses at parse, exit 2:
  unexpected token '|' in expression position

Infix `||` is retired; concatenation is the `[$concat …]` built-in
(grammar.ebnf, the [132]-[134] retirement note). The expression is also not a
path, so `cx select` would refuse it even in CX spelling — the same
surface-scope fact xpath31-030 pins.
#]]
 ]

 [# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    §5. Documented divergences — CX refuses what XPath 3.1 accepts
    ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #]

 [case id=xpath31-100-general-comparison-divergence
  [tag [#
xpath31-divergence
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<users><user age="20"/><user age="25"/><user age="30"/></users>
#]]
  [path [#
//users/@age=25
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0100
#]]
  [expect-diff [#
Saxon returns 'true' — the general comparison quantifies existentially over
the sequence.

CX refuses at parse, exit 2:
  unexpected token '=' in expression position

Infix comparison is retired at every position (grammar.ebnf [132]-[134]
retirement); predicates are homoiconic prefix forms. CX has no general
comparison at all: `[= …]` is a value comparison over two operands, and
existential intent is written explicitly (cxpath_alignment.md, sequence
flatness and the typed-value model).
#]]
 ]

 [case id=xpath31-101-xpath-for-construct-divergence
  [tag [#
xpath31-divergence
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<users><user email="a@x"/></users>
#]]
  [path [#
for $x in //user return $x/@email
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0100
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
a@x
#]]
  [expect-diff [#
Saxon returns 'a@x'.

CX refuses, exit 2:
  PATH must be a CXPath path expression ($doc/…, /… or //…)

FLWOR has no CXPath production: iteration is the `[?for …]` directive, a
program construct, not a path construct (cxpath_alignment.md, no FLWOR).
#]]
 ]

 [case id=xpath31-102-xs-type-divergence
  [tag [#
xpath31-divergence
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<doc/>
#]]
  [path [#
42 instance of xs:integer
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0100
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
true
#]]
  [expect-diff [#
Saxon returns 'true'.

CX refuses, exit 2:
  PATH must be a CXPath path expression ($doc/…, /… or //…)

`instance of` and the whole XSD type lattice are absent; CX's kind surface is
its own and coercion is the prefix `[cast $v :int]` (cxpath_alignment.md, no
XSD type layer).
#]]
 ]

 [case id=xpath31-103-namespace-decl-in-expression-divergence
  [tag [#
xpath31-divergence
#]]
  [input-xml [#
<doc xmlns:ns="http://example.org"><ns:foo/></doc>
#]]
  [path [#
declare namespace ns = 'http://example.org'; //ns:foo
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0100
#]]
  [expect-saxon [#
<ns:foo xmlns:ns="http://example.org"/>
#]]
  [expect-diff [#
Saxon evaluates successfully after the prolog declaration.

CX refuses at parse, exit 2:
  unexpected character ; (byte 0x3b)

A CXPath expression carries no namespace environment of its own: prefixes
resolve against the DOCUMENT's in-scope xmlns map (cxpath_alignment.md, no
in-expression namespace declarations). The conversion preserves the
declaration — `cx --from=xml --to=cx` on this input yields
`[doc xmlns:ns=http://example.org [ns:foo]]` — so `//ns:foo` alone resolves;
only the prolog spelling is refused.
#]]
 ]
]
